==English==
{{wikipedia|Feeder (disambiguation)}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|feedere}}, {{m|enm|federe}}, {{m|enm|fedare}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|feed|er|id2=agent noun}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfidɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-feeder.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːdə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who [[feed]]s, or gives food to another.
## The [[participant]] in [[feederism]] who feeds the other (the [[feedee]]).
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2010|author=Niall Richardson|title=Transgressive Bodies
|passage=Often similes such as 'soft as velvet' or 'fluffy like a cloud' will be employed and the '''feeder''' will describe how he feels he can be lost in the enveloping folds of soft flesh.}}
# One who [[feed]]s, or takes in food.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|II|v|text=The patch is kind enough, but a huge '''feeder''',<br>Snail-slow in profit, and he sleeps by day<br>More than the wild-cat; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1871|author=George Miller Beard|title=Eating and Drinking|passage=There are many who are dietarians in theory, but liberal '''feeders''' in practice. They suppose or maintain that it is a duty to deny oneself of all luxuries at the table, but practically they take the best that they can get.}}
# One who, or that which, {{l|en|feed#Verb|feeds}} material into something (especially a {{l|en|machine}}).
#: {{cot|en|feed#Noun|feedbin|hopper}}
#: {{hypo|en|barfeed|barfeeder}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=Thomas E. Lightburn|title=The Shield and the Shark|page=173
|passage=When the claxon sounded they immediately stopped what they were doing and uncovered the Oerlikon. Paddy, who was ammunition '''feeder''', stood by while Jock trained the 20mm gun around.}}
# That which is used to [[feed]].
#: {{ux|en|a bird '''feeder'''}}
# A [[tributary]] [[stream]], especially of a [[canal]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1827|author=w:Conrad Malte-Brun|title=Universal Geography, or A Description of All the Parts of the World, on a New Plan|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Adam Black|section=volume 6, book 101, {{gbooks|LkYNAQAAIAAJ|285|feeder}}
|passage=The surface of the Balaton and the surrounding marshes is not less than 24 German square miles, or 384 English square miles; its principal '''feeder''' is the Szala, but all the water it receives appears inconsiderable relatively to its superficial extent, and the quantity lost in evaporation.}}
# A [[branch line]] of a [[railway]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1942|month=May-June|author=Charles E. Lee|title=The Brampton Railway|journal=Railway Magazine|page=140|text=This line, described as a valuable '''feeder''' to the Newcastle & Carlisle Railway, was opened on July 8, 1836, and superseded the older wagonway.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1959|month=August|title=Talking of Trains: The costs of transport|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=346|text=Another factor to be allowed for in establishing the remunerativeness of a service was its value as a '''feeder''' to the rest of the system.}}
# A [[transmission]] [[line]] that feeds the electricity for an [[electricity]] [[substation]], or for a [[transmitter]].
# {{lb|en|education}} {{short for|en|feeder school}}
# {{lb|en|shipbuilding|navigation}} A [[feeder ship]].
# {{lb|en|US|legal}} A [[judge]] whose law clerks are often selected to become clerks for the [[Supreme Court]].
# {{lb|en|baseball|slang|archaic|1800s}} The [[pitcher]].
# {{lb|en|video games|derogatory}} A player whose character is killed by the opposing player or team more than once, deliberately or through lack of skills and experience, thus helping the opposing side.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} One who [[abet]]s another.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|II|iv|lines=815–818|text=Go with me; if you like upon report<br>The soil, the profit, and this kind of life,<br>I will your very faithful '''feeder''' be,<br>And buy it with your gold right suddenly. }}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[parasite]].

